WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:28.980
Kenya's charcoal ban drove a 130% surge in Ugandan imports, shifting deforestation across the border. This is unintended consequences, episode 170. What officials thought the forest needed, how the stop was enforced, how the kilns moved, where the trade sits now, and what a restriction like this asks of anyone writing the next one. On the 24th of February 2018, the Kenyan government announced a moratorium on logging and charcoal production in public and community forests.

00:00:28.980 --> 00:00:33.060
Demestic charcoal prices rose 36% almost immediately.

00:00:33.580 --> 00:00:36.660
The measure had been written to slow forest loss inside Kenya.

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:38.900
The cutting and the trade did not stop.

00:00:39.400 --> 00:00:39.840
They moved.

00:00:40.360 --> 00:00:42.900
The damage officials were looking at was not abstract.

00:00:43.600 --> 00:00:47.660
Long-running deforestation had already removed large areas of indigenous forest.

00:00:48.240 --> 00:00:53.520
Charcoal was still the primary cooking fuel for more than 80% of households, so any restriction

00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:55.500
carried an immediate cost at the stove.

00:00:55.500 --> 00:01:01.920
The February 2018 moratorium followed earlier task force reviews that had documented illegal logging,

00:01:02.320 --> 00:01:07.900
weak enforcement, and revenue losses inside state forests. A nationwide production ban looked

00:01:07.900 --> 00:01:13.300
from that record like a clear and enforceable signal. Remaining woodlands would get time to recover.

00:01:13.780 --> 00:01:18.820
The measure also sat in a familiar pattern of short-term logging suspensions, which made it feel

00:01:18.820 --> 00:01:24.620
proportionate to the damage people could already see. The arithmetic on paper was straightforward. If

00:01:24.620 --> 00:01:29.760
Charcoal output inside Kenya fell by the targeted volume, standing trees would remain standing,

00:01:30.100 --> 00:01:33.100
and the carbon stored in those trees would stay out of the atmosphere.

00:01:33.820 --> 00:01:38.160
Officials also expected higher-rate side prices to nudge some households toward other

00:01:38.160 --> 00:01:41.000
fuels over time, even if that switch took years.

00:01:41.580 --> 00:01:46.700
The Task Force report that preceded the ban had cataloged specific failures, unlicensed

00:01:46.700 --> 00:01:50.060
pits, understaffed checkpoints, and lost stumpage fees.

00:01:50.060 --> 00:01:54.740
A blanket production stop looked like the cleanest way to reset the system before anyone

00:01:54.740 --> 00:01:56.060
tried to write new rules.

00:01:56.939 --> 00:02:00.559
The moratorium took effect at once across public and community forests.

00:02:01.359 --> 00:02:04.939
Forest service checkpoints and licensing freezes were the tools on hand.

00:02:05.459 --> 00:02:10.199
Early monitoring recorded higher roadside prices and less visible charcoal production inside

00:02:10.199 --> 00:02:10.579
Kenya.

00:02:11.259 --> 00:02:14.219
Supporters pointed to those signs and said the policy was biting.

00:02:14.779 --> 00:02:19.139
The Ministry of Environment and Forestry set up a task force to review forest governance

00:02:19.139 --> 00:02:21.359
and to recommend longer-term rules.

00:02:21.879 --> 00:02:25.559
Inside the charcoal trade, skeptics warned that demand would not vanish,

00:02:25.919 --> 00:02:29.059
and that supply would move to neighboring countries with lighter controls.

00:02:29.639 --> 00:02:30.799
Those warnings were noted.

00:02:31.279 --> 00:02:33.559
They did not change the nationwide scope of the ban.

00:02:34.159 --> 00:02:36.759
Enforcement teams went to Kenyan production sites first,

00:02:37.259 --> 00:02:40.519
because those sites were the ones existing staff and vehicles could reach.

00:02:41.179 --> 00:02:44.119
Within the first month, legal charcoal movement inside Kenya

00:02:44.119 --> 00:02:46.279
dropped sharply at the monitored checkpoints.

00:02:46.279 --> 00:02:50.239
On paper, the quantity reduction the ban had aimed for was there.

00:02:50.799 --> 00:02:55.459
The same teams had no mandate and no resources to inspect production on the Uganda inside

00:02:55.459 --> 00:02:55.959
of the border.

00:02:56.699 --> 00:02:59.279
The policy's geographic reach stopped at the frontier.

00:03:00.446 --> 00:03:04.926
Within weeks, commercial buyers turned to legal and gray market charcoal arriving by truck

00:03:04.926 --> 00:03:06.406
from Uganda and Tanzania.

00:03:07.006 --> 00:03:12.306
The IOP study recorded that jump and recorded charcoal imports from Uganda over the first six

00:03:12.306 --> 00:03:13.626
months after the moratorium.

00:03:13.626 --> 00:03:13.746
William.

00:03:14.346 --> 00:03:17.126
Truck volumes rose at border points such as Boussia.

00:03:17.766 --> 00:03:20.646
Congestion rose with them, and so did informal payments.

00:03:21.366 --> 00:03:26.446
In Uganda, where regulatory oversight remained weaker, new production sites opened to meet

00:03:26.446 --> 00:03:27.186
Kenyan demand.

00:03:27.826 --> 00:03:30.286
Forest loss was displaced rather than eliminated.

00:03:30.906 --> 00:03:35.066
The same study found that net carbon emissions from the regional charcoal trade increased

00:03:35.066 --> 00:03:37.206
once that displaced production was counted.

00:03:37.826 --> 00:03:39.826
Prices inside Kenya stayed elevated.

00:03:39.826 --> 00:03:44.546
added. Some households moved toward fuels that were even more expensive or less efficient.

00:03:45.186 --> 00:03:50.206
Cross-border traders organized more systematic routes. Those routes became the gray market

00:03:50.206 --> 00:03:55.666
networks later mapped by the global initiative against transnational organized crime. A ban

00:03:55.666 --> 00:04:00.386
drawn around one country's forests had rewarded producers who could work just outside Kenyan

00:04:00.386 --> 00:04:05.906
jurisdiction. Overall, wood extraction stayed largely intact. Households that still cooked

00:04:05.906 --> 00:04:10.546
on charcoal did not cut the total amount they needed. The price rise changed the source

00:04:10.546 --> 00:04:15.366
of supply, not the need. Each additional truck that crossed at Boussia carried roughly

00:04:15.366 --> 00:04:20.446
the same volume of charcoal a Kenyan kiln would have produced. The kiln inside Kenya sat

00:04:20.446 --> 00:04:26.046
idle. A new kiln operated across the line in Uganda. The carbon accounting followed the

00:04:26.046 --> 00:04:31.246
same path. Trees cut in Uganda released the same tonnage of carbon dioxide, whether the

00:04:31.246 --> 00:04:33.226
Cook lived in Nairobi or in Kampala.

00:04:33.786 --> 00:04:38.826
When the IOP researchers added the Ugandan emissions to the Kenyan figures, the combined

00:04:38.826 --> 00:04:40.406
total rows rather than fell.

00:04:40.966 --> 00:04:45.526
The higher Kenyan price also created a margin large enough to cover transport costs and

00:04:45.526 --> 00:04:46.706
informal border fees.

00:04:47.266 --> 00:04:49.926
That margin locked the new supply chain into place.

00:04:50.546 --> 00:04:54.806
Traders who had moved small lots scaled up because the price difference more than offset

00:04:54.806 --> 00:04:56.806
the added distance and the added risk.

00:04:56.806 --> 00:05:02.693
The original moratorium was later adjusted, and some logging restrictions were partly lifted.

00:05:03.233 --> 00:05:06.653
Charcoal imports from Uganda and Tanzania stayed high anyway.

00:05:07.193 --> 00:05:13.553
World Bank trade data for 2023 and 2024 still show those two countries as the leading recorded

00:05:13.553 --> 00:05:18.773
suppliers of wood charcoal to Kenya, with values in the tens of millions of dollars each year.

00:05:19.373 --> 00:05:23.673
Enforcement moved toward border monitoring and toward attempts at regional cooperation.

00:05:24.333 --> 00:05:26.153
The price difference kept the trade alive.

00:05:26.153 --> 00:05:30.213
5. No comprehensive reversal of the cross-border flow has been achieved.

00:05:30.573 --> 00:05:35.693
The environmental pressure now sits more visibly in the source countries. Later attempts to

00:05:35.693 --> 00:05:41.413
harmonize rules across the East African community ran into differing national priorities and differing

00:05:41.413 --> 00:05:47.453
enforcement capacity. The price gap remained. When Kenya restricted charcoal production inside

00:05:47.453 --> 00:05:52.253
its own borders, the immediate price signal rewarded any supplier who could move production

00:05:52.253 --> 00:05:57.473
across the nearest line with weaker rules. A single country quantity restriction on a widely

00:05:57.473 --> 00:06:02.873
used good will pull supply from next door unless those neighbors adopt matching limits at the same

00:06:02.873 --> 00:06:08.953
moment. An account of only domestic forest cover, after a ban like this, can hide continued or

00:06:08.953 --> 00:06:13.673
increased extraction once the trade figures are in the room. The same shape shows up whenever

00:06:13.673 --> 00:06:19.113
a national rule meets a good people still have to buy every day, and a border where the rule does not.

00:06:19.113 --> 00:06:24.473
If you want the longer watch on how environmental rules travel, environmental intelligence

00:06:24.473 --> 00:06:26.913
follows that governance question past the headline.

00:06:27.493 --> 00:06:32.513
How might regulators today test whether a proposed national restriction will simply relocate

00:06:32.513 --> 00:06:34.053
the activity they aim to curb?

